      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the areas of both the square and the circle, and then determine the difference in their areas.\n\n1. **Calculate the side length of the square:**\n   The length of the diagonal \\( d \\) of a square with side length \\( s \\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   d = s\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   Given that the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we can solve for \\( s \\):\n   \\[\n   5 = s\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the square:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of a square with side length \\( s \\) is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = s^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( s = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the radius of the circle:**\n   The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the circle:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of a circle with radius \\( r \\) is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( r = 2.5 \\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n   Using the approximation \\( \\pi \\approx 3.14159 \\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14159 \\approx 19.63495 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the difference in area:**\n   The difference in area between the circle and the square is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Difference} = A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\approx 19.63495 - 12.5 \\approx 7.13495\n   \\]\n   Rounding to the nearest tenth:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{7.1}\n   \\]",
